diff --git a/pages/cdn64.en.html b/pages/cdn64.en.html
index 6eb68a2..564bc9b 100644
--- a/pages/cdn64.en.html
+++ b/pages/cdn64.en.html
@@ -14,7 +14,7 @@
 <p>In your home network, the local reverse proxy of Node64 is called <strong>PROXY64</strong>; <strong>CDN64</strong> is the server part at IPv64.net. You manage CDN64 at <a href="https://ipv64.net/cdn64" rel="noopener">ipv64.net/cdn64</a>, in Node64 and via the <a href="{{u "cdn64/api"}}">CDN64 API</a>. Without logging in, <a href="https://ipv64.net/cdn64.php?lang=en" rel="noopener">ipv64.net/cdn64</a> shows an overview with the three modes.</p>
 
 <h2 id="funktionsweise">How CDN64 works</h2>
-<p>A name you switch on in CDN64 (e.g. <code>cloud.deinedomain.ipv64.net</code>) points to the edge servers of its tunnel in DNS – your account class decides how many (see <a href="#server-je-tunnel">servers per tunnel</a>). A visitor lands on one of the servers. The server recognises from the name where the connection belongs and passes it on.</p>
+<p>A name you switch on in CDN64 (e.g. <code>cloud.deinedomain.ipv64.net</code>) points to its edge servers in DNS – your account class decides how many (see <a href="#server-je-name">servers per name</a>). A visitor lands on one of the servers. The server recognises from the name where the connection belongs and passes it on.</p>
 <pre class="ascii nocopy" aria-label="Diagram: visitors reach the edge servers, Node64 keeps a tunnel to each, in mode 3 the servers go directly to the target">
  Visitor (IPv4/IPv6)
         │  DNS: A/AAAA of your account's edge servers (TTL 10 s)
@@ -37,18 +37,18 @@
 <ul>
 	<li><strong>Several servers via DNS:</strong> DNS returns the addresses of your account's edge servers. From two servers on, one is in Nuremberg and one in Falkenstein; visitors are spread across them automatically.</li>
 	<li><strong>A server fails:</strong> if an edge server does not report to IPv64.net for 45 seconds and does not answer a direct check either, IPv64.net removes its addresses from DNS – as long as another server is reachable. Until then the other servers of your account carry the load. If it stays away for more than 3 minutes (so not just a restart), another server stands in for the accounts it is assigned to, preferably at the same location: DNS gets that server's addresses, and Node64 additionally connects to it within a minute – without interrupting the connections to the other servers. If your account has only one server (Free), the name is unreachable until then. Once the server is back for 30 seconds, the previous assignment applies again. A failover is visible on ipv64.net/cdn64 (yellow “failover” badge at the name, note at the tunnel), in Node64 (PROXY64 dashboard card and badge “srv08 → srv09” in the <em>CDN64</em> column), in the node's log and in the events of the names.</li>
-	<li><strong>Parallel connections:</strong> depending on your account class, Node64 opens several parallel tunnel connections to each server (Business up to 2, Company up to 4, your choice per tunnel; see <a href="#server-je-tunnel">servers per tunnel</a>). The servers hand new visitors to the least loaded connection.</li>
+	<li><strong>Parallel connections:</strong> depending on your account class, Node64 opens several parallel tunnel connections to each server (Business up to 2, Company up to 4, your choice per name; see <a href="#server-je-name">servers per name</a>). The servers hand new visitors to the least loaded connection.</li>
 	<li><strong>Several tunnels for one name:</strong> a name can be assigned to several tunnels (i.e. several Node64 instances). The order is the priority: the first tunnel that is currently connected serves the name. This is a fallback, not load balancing between nodes. You set the list via the <a href="{{u "cdn64/api"}}#name-aendern">API</a> (field <code>tunnels</code>).</li>
 </ul>
-<h3 id="server-je-tunnel">Servers per tunnel</h3>
-<p>Not every tunnel uses all edge servers. Your account class decides how many: Free 1, Home / Supporter 2, Business 1 or 2 (your choice), Company 1 to all (your choice; the list grows with the number of servers, “all” includes future servers). In addition, Business (1–2) and Company (1–4) choose the connections per server, e.g. 1 server with 1 connection or 3 servers with 2 connections each. Free (1 server, 1 connection) and Supporter (2 servers, 1 connection each) are fixed. The values apply per Node64 tunnel to all names of that tunnel. Names in mode 3 (without a tunnel) use the highest number of the class.</p>
+<h3 id="server-je-name">Servers per name</h3>
+<p>Not every name uses all edge servers. Your account class decides how many: Free 1, Home / Supporter 2, Business 1 or 2 (your choice), Company 1 to all (your choice; the list grows with the number of servers, “all” includes future servers). In addition, Business (1–2) and Company (1–4) choose the connections per server, e.g. 1 server with 1 connection or 3 servers with 2 connections each. Free (1 server, 1 connection) and Supporter (2 servers, 1 connection each) are fixed. The values apply per name (in Node64 per proxy host for all its names), e.g. name A with 1 server and 1 connection, name B with 4 servers and 4 connections each; in mode 3 only the number of servers. The DNS of a name lists only its servers. The tunnel of a node connects to the servers of all its names, per server with the highest number of connections of the names there; the names share the connections of a server.</p>
 <ul>
-	<li><strong>Assignment:</strong> IPv64.net assigns the servers automatically – one per location first, each time the one with the fewest tunnels. The assignment stays fixed until the number changes. Node64 shows the servers a tunnel currently uses in the <em>CDN64</em> column of the proxy hosts.</li>
-	<li><strong>Choosing servers and connections (Business, Company):</strong> in Node64 under <em>PROXY64 → Proxy hosts</em> in the <em>CDN64</em> column (button with the number of servers) or via the <a href="{{u "cdn64/api"}}#server">API</a>, per tunnel. IPv64.net then updates the DNS of the names of this tunnel; Node64 connects to the new servers within a minute, connections to the remaining ones keep running.</li>
-	<li><strong>Several tunnels for one name:</strong> DNS names the servers of the first tunnel. The backup tunnels additionally connect to these servers so they can take over.</li>
-	<li><strong>Class changed:</strong> the new number applies within a few minutes; the tunnel keeps the servers assigned first.</li>
-	<li><strong>Old DNS answer at the visitor:</strong> if a visitor still reaches a server the tunnel no longer uses, that server passes them on to a server with your tunnel.</li>
-	<li id="lastausgleich"><strong>Load balancing (emergency brake):</strong> if an edge server stays above 70 % of its capacity for 15 minutes and another one is below 40 %, IPv64.net moves one tunnel with noticeable traffic there – at most one per quarter of an hour, never during a server failure and never for Company with “all”. The new server is added first; the old one is dropped only when your tunnel is up on the new one, so there is no outage. A moved tunnel then stays on its servers for 24 hours. The move shows in the events of the names (“Lastausgleich: srv08 → srv10”) and in the Node64 log.</li>
+	<li><strong>Assignment:</strong> IPv64.net assigns the servers automatically – one per location first, then servers other names of the same node already use (fewer connections), otherwise the one with the fewest assignments. The assignment stays fixed until the number changes. Node64 shows the servers a proxy host currently uses in its row in the <em>CDN64</em> column.</li>
+	<li><strong>Choosing servers and connections (Business, Company):</strong> in Node64 under <em>PROXY64 → Proxy hosts</em> in the <em>CDN64</em> column (button with the number of servers in the row of the proxy host) or via the <a href="{{u "cdn64/api"}}#server">API</a>, per name. IPv64.net then updates the DNS of the name; Node64 connects to the new servers within a minute, connections to the remaining ones keep running.</li>
+	<li><strong>Several tunnels for one name:</strong> all tunnels of the name connect to its servers so a backup can take over.</li>
+	<li><strong>Class changed:</strong> the new number applies within a few minutes; the name keeps the servers assigned first.</li>
+	<li><strong>Old DNS answer at the visitor:</strong> if a visitor still reaches a server the name no longer uses, that server passes them on to a server with your tunnel.</li>
+	<li id="lastausgleich"><strong>Load balancing (emergency brake):</strong> if an edge server stays above 70 % of its capacity for 15 minutes and another one is below 40 %, IPv64.net moves one name (mode 1/2) with noticeable traffic there – at most one per quarter of an hour, never during a server failure and never for Company with “all”. The new server is added first; the old one is dropped only when your tunnel is up on the new one, so there is no outage. A moved name then stays on its servers for 24 hours. The move shows in the events of the names (“Lastausgleich: srv08 → srv10”) and in the Node64 log.</li>
 </ul>
 
 <h2 id="modi">The three modes</h2>
